in this archive:

https://ballyalley.com/program_downloads/2000_baud_programs/arcadian/programs_a-h/programs_a-h.html#ArtilleryDuelJohnPerkinsAB

Artillery Code (Commented - Changes made to the structure layout/look)

I think that I have this program correctly restructured so that it's a bit easier to read.  Sometimes I get confused with how an IF statement works in AstroBASIC.  I think that if an IF statement is not true, then the entire rest of the line is skipped.  In this way, if several IF statements are on the same line, than (I THINK) it acts like an ELSE IF structure.

Now that the program is a little easier to read, I should be able to comment it a bit easier.  Also, figuring out variables becomes easier too.  For instance, it's easy to see that W = Wind.  It seems, from a quick glance, that Angle and Bags of Powder are pulled from the @ array based on the variables A and B.  However, like many AstroBASIC programs, which are limited to only 26 variables (A-Z), this program probably reuses variables, so you can usually not depend on looking at a BASIC listing and presuming that A = Angle, B = Bags and W= Wind all of the time.

I'm going to pause now and continue looking at this BASIC listing over the next few days.  Some of these BOX commands sure look complicated (look at line 150, for example).  Working my way through this program is gonna be FUN!


